To use a custom trust store with a custom password without setting the trust store password via a system property, you can load the trust store programmatically within your Java application. Below is a sample code snippet demonstrating how to do this:

```java
import javax.net.ssl.*;
import java.io.FileInputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.security.*;
import java.security.cert.CertificateException;

public class CustomTrustStore {

    public void useCustomTrustStore() {
        try {
            // Load the trust store: the trust store should contain the server's certificate
            KeyStore myTrustStore = KeyStore.getInstance("JKS");
            FileInputStream myKeys = new FileInputStream("truststore.jks"); //"truststore.jks" is your trust store file

            // Load the trust store, the default password is "changeit"
            String password = "password"; // use your truststore password here
            myTrustStore.load(myKeys, password.toCharArray());

            // Create a trust manager factory with the custom truststore
            TrustManagerFactory tmf = TrustManagerFactory.getInstance(TrustManagerFactory.getDefaultAlgorithm());
            tmf.init(myTrustStore);

            // Create an SSL context that uses the created trust manager
            SSLContext sslContext = SSLContext.getInstance("TLS");
            sslContext.init(null, tmf.getTrustManagers(), new SecureRandom());

            // Set the default Socket Factory to use the custom truststore
            HttpsURLConnection.setDefaultSSLSocketFactory(sslContext.getSocketFactory());
        } catch (KeyStoreException | IOException | CertificateException | NoSuchAlgorithmException | KeyManagementException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

Please remember to replace "truststore.jks" and "password" with your real truststore path and password.

This code works by loading the trust store and initializing a `TrustManagerFactory` with it. The `TrustManagerFactory` is then used to initialize an `SSLContext`. 

Finally, the `SSLContext` is used to set the default `SSLSocketFactory`. This will make all `HttpsURLConnection`s use the custom trust store when establishing SSL connections.

Note: This method only affects `HttpsURLConnection`. If you're using a different method to establish SSL connections, you may need a different approach.

