Creating fluent async method in TypeScript could be a little tricky since fluent methods require the instance of the class as return type, but async methods return a Promise. To provide a fluent interface with async methods in TypeScript, you may need to introduce some more advanced concepts like returning a Proxy instead of the class instance. Here's an example of how it could look like:

```typescript
class MyClass {
   private value: number;

   constructor() {
      this.value = 0;
   }

   async setValue(val: number): Promise<MyClass> {
      this.value = val;
      return this;
   }
}

function FluentAsync<T>(instance: T): T {
    return new Proxy(instance, {
        get(target, prop, receiver) {
            if(typeof target[prop] === 'function') {
                return async (...args: any[]) => {
                    const result = await target[prop](...args);
                    return result === target ? receiver : result;
                };
            }
            return Reflect.get(target, prop, receiver);
        }
    });
}

const instance = FluentAsync(new MyClass());

(async () => {
    await instance.setValue(10).setValue(20);
    console.log(instance);  
})();
```

In this example, a Proxy is used to wrap the class instance with a get trap, which enables overwriting the behavior of getting the object's properties. If a function is called on a wrapped object (a function name is detected), the get trap returns a new function that automatically wraps the function result with FluentAsync if it's the instance of a class. This way, whenever you call an async method on an instance of the class and await it, you get the FluentAsync wrapped instance back, allowing you to chain calls fluently. 

However, the async nature of the function makes the fluent interface slightly awkward because you need to use `await` keyword every time you call a method (to get the Promise resolved), which may cause readability issues. There's no perfect way to hide Promise from a method and keep it fluent, because async method by its nature requires to be waited on to produce results.

