To avoid nested `<p>` tags, you can check if the content is already wrapped in a `<p>` tag before wrapping it again.

Here is one way to do it:

```php
<?php

if(!empty($recordData['text'])) {

  $text = $recordData['text'];

  if (strpos($text, '<p>') === false) {
    $text = '<p class="small">' . $text . '</p>';
  }

  echo $text;

}

?>
```

This checks if `<p>` exists in the text using `strpos()`. If not, it will wrap the text in a `<p>` tag with the class.

Alternatively, you could use DOMDocument to manipulate the HTML more robustly:

```php 
$doc = new DOMDocument();
$doc->loadHTML($recordData['text']);

$paragraphs = $doc->getElementsByTagName('p');
if($paragraphs->length == 0) {
  $p = $doc->createElement('p');
  $p->setAttribute('class', 'small');
  $doc->appendChild($p); 
} else {
  $p = $paragraphs->item(0);
}

echo $doc->saveHTML($p);
```

This loads the HTML, gets any existing `<p>` tags, and adds the class to the first one if needed.

