```csharp
public class DateTimeConverter : JsonConverter<DateTime>
{
    public override DateTime Read(ref Utf8JsonReader reader, Type typeToConvert, JsonSerializerOptions options)
    {
        return DateTime.Parse(reader.GetString());
    }

    public override void Write(Utf8JsonWriter writer, DateTime value, JsonSerializerOptions options)
    {
        writer.WriteStringValue(value.ToUniversalTime().ToString("yyyy'-'MM'-'dd'T'HH':'mm':'ss'.'fff'Z'"));
    }
}
```

In this example, the `Read` method is used to convert a `string` to a `DateTime`, and the `Write` method is used to convert a `DateTime` to a `string`.

To use this converter, you'll need to add it to the `JsonSerializerOptions` that you use when calling `JsonSerializer.Serialize` or `JsonSerializer.Deserialize`:

```csharp
var options = new JsonSerializerOptions();
options.Converters.Add(new DateTimeConverter());

var json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(myObject, options);
var myObject = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<MyObjectType>(json, options);
```

In this case, the `DateTimeConverter` will be used for all `DateTime` properties of the object being serialized or deserialized. Note that the date time format used in the converter is the ISO 8601 format. You can change that to whatever format you prefer.

